{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c597","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e75-bbc6-7500-a9cb-f70149b63e89"},"sequence":294,"recorded_at":1780531400000583,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"585d5240-0b82-4f46-b1ce-65f22684f16a"},"task_id":"585d5240-0b82-4f46-b1ce-65f22684f16a","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"585d5240-0b82-4f46-b1ce-65f22684f16a","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2xdt8t/TASK-260830-2xdt8t_review-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2xdt8t — reviewer verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED\n2|\n3|Reviewer run `RUN-260904-cd73df` (Opus 5). Change Request `CR-TASK-260830-2xdt8t-1`\n4|revision 1. Verdict: **changes requested → `to-dev`**.\n5|\n6|## 0. What was actually reviewed, and the `repository_delta=empty` question\n7|\n8|The Change Request snapshot reports `repository_delta=empty` with a zero-path\n9|patch (`sha256:e3b0c442…855`, the empty-string digest). That is a snapshot\n10|artifact, not a producer failure, and I verified it rather than accepting the\n11|prompt's assertion:\n12|\n13|```\n14|git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}                 -> 8ec7a392e176c1dd25795b3be2d4a302213b5588\n15|git rev-parse b5cf5b5…244^{tree}          -> 8ec7a392e176c1dd25795b3be2d4a302213b5588   (candidate == base == HEAD)\n16|git show --stat b5cf5b5                   -> 5 files changed, 1379 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)\n17|git verify-commit b5cf5b5                 -> Good \"git\" signature for oparin@me.com (ECDSA SHA256:V6JiKG7J…)\n18|git status --short                        -> (clean)\n19|git log --oneline -2                      -> b5cf5b5 (this leaf) on 57afcc6 (checkpoint)\n20|```\n21|\n22|The base OID **is** this leaf's commit, so the snapshot diffed the delivered\n23|commit against itself. The real reviewable delta is `b5cf5b5`: a signed commit,\n24|exactly one past the checkpoint, clean worktree, not pushed — the delivery shape\n25|this non-final leaf was asked for. **The producer did change repository files.**\n26|Everything below is judged against `b5cf5b5`.\n27|\n28|Baseline before any mutation: `go test ./...` green, 14 packages, 28.4s wall.\n29|\n30|## 1. Verdict summary\n31|\n32|Five blocking findings. Two of them (F1, F3) are the exact negative-evidence\n33|shapes this board handed the producer by name in the task notes.\n34|\n35|| # | Finding | Shape |\n36|| --- | --- | --- |\n37|| F1 | Registry hands out and retains live interior state; the **drift gate is bypassable** | accessor does not preserve validation |\n38|| F2 | Drift \"one member at a time\" claim is false for 2 of 5 record members | pin exercises the length, not the member |\n39|| F3 | `TestCheckProviderDescriptorGenerationBounds` proves nothing about the bound it names | pin exercises the parameter, not the subject |\n40|| F4 | Four guards have **zero** negative coverage; 12 of 32 mutants survive | unmeasured refusal domain |\n41|| F5 | §6.2 negative arm is bound to nothing; ownership registry and README still claim the positive-only discharge | stale capability claim |\n42|\n43|Both producer decisions the board asked me to adjudicate are **upheld** (§3).\n44|The `no new ownership claimed` posture is **mostly honest** (§4).\n45|\n46|## 2. Blocking findings\n47|\n48|### F1 — the deliverable's drift gate is bypassable through the package's own accessor\n49|\n50|`Registration` carries two slices (`ProtocolVersions`, `Platforms`).\n51|`Registry.Resolve` returns the struct by value, so the slice **headers** are\n52|copied and the **backing arrays are shared with the registry**.\n53|`RegisterExternal` stores the caller's `observed` record verbatim, so the caller\n54|keeps a live handle on an admitted record. `New` sorts one protocol slice and\n55|assigns it to **both** built-ins, so they share one array.\n56|\n57|Confirmed, three ways, driving the real exported API\n58|(`.temp/TASK-260830-2xdt8t/review-aliasing-probe.log`):\n59|\n60|```\n61|before: platforms=[linux macos wsl2] protocols=[1.0.0]\n62|after : platforms=[windows macos wsl2] protocols=[9.9.9]\n63|ALIASING CONFIRMED: registry platform list mutated through Resolve() accessor\n64|ALIASING CONFIRMED: registry protocol list mutated through Resolve() accessor\n65|\n66|admitted record after caller mutation: platforms=[windows] protocols=[1.99.0]\n67|POST-ADMISSION DRIFT CONFIRMED: caller mutated an admitted record without passing any gate\n68|\n69|conpty protocols after mutating tmux's list: [1.5.0 1.1.0]\n70|SHARED BACKING ARRAY CONFIRMED: both built-ins share one protocol slice\n71|```\n72|\n73|This is not cosmetic. `equalRecord` compares the candidate against the *stored*\n74|record, and the stored record is the same array the caller holds, so an\n75|attacker-influenced adapter that mutates in place converts real drift into a\n76|benign duplicate. Proven end-to-end\n77|(`.temp/TASK-260830-2xdt8t/zz_review_probe2_test.go`):\n78|\n79|```\n80|DRIFT GATE BYPASSED: a changed protocol list reported as duplicate, not drift:\n81|  terminal backend refused: terminal_backend_ambiguous for com.example.term at duplicate backend_id\n82|registry now believes it admitted protocols=[1.4.0]\n83|```\n84|\n85|`terminal_backend_implementation_drift` is the wire code this task exists to\n86|emit. It emitted `terminal_backend_ambiguous` instead, and the registry silently\n87|adopted the changed protocol list as its admitted record.\n88|\n89|The board's task notes name this class verbatim: *\"Validation that does not\n90|survive the package's own accessors is not validation — a preceding Story\n91|shipped a shallow copy whose accessor handed out live interior state.\"*\n92|It is also an architecture-fit failure: this repository already has the\n93|convention and does not use it here — `internal/catalog/catalog.go` clones every\n94|accessor return (`cloneSource`, `cloneContracts`, `cloneOperation`,\n95|`cloneCapability`, `cloneEvent`, `cloneError`), `internal/specpin/pin.go` has\n96|`cloneContracts`/`cloneContract`, and `internal/config/validation.go` — the file\n97|this leaf edited — has `cloneStrings` and `cloneAnyMap`.\n98|\n99|Fix: deep-copy on ingress in `New`/`RegisterExternal` and on egress in\n100|`Resolve`, and pin it with a test that mutates every returned slice and\n101|re-resolves. `IDs()` is already safe (freshly built).\n102|\n103|### F2 — the drift gate is unproven for two of the five members it compares\n104|\n105|The producer's board note claims *\"drift changes one record member at a time\"*.\n106|Two of the five do not:\n107|\n108|- **M12** — delete the `ProtocolVersions` member-by-member loop from\n109|  `equalRecord`: **full suite stays green.**\n110|- **M13** — delete the `Platforms` member-by-member loop: **full suite stays green.**\n111|\n112|`TestDriftIsRefused`'s `protocol list` case goes `[\"1.0.0\"] → [\"1.0.0\",\"1.1.0\"]`\n113|and its `platforms` case goes `[linux] → [linux macos]`. Both change the slice\n114|**length**, so only the `len(...) != len(...)` guard is exercised; the element\n115|comparison is dead weight as far as the suite is concerned. Same-length,\n116|different-member drift — the realistic adapter-swap case, and precisely what F1\n117|exploits — has no coverage.\n118|\n119|The three scalar members are genuinely pinned: M09 (drop `Kind`), M10 (drop\n120|`ImplementationVersion`), M11 (drop `ExecutableDigest`) are all killed.\n121|\n122|Fix: add same-length drift cases (`[\"1.0.0\"] → [\"1.1.0\"]`, `[linux] → [macos]`).\n123|\n124|### F3 — the §7.A generation bound test proves the mismatch, not the bound\n125|\n126|`TestCheckProviderDescriptorGenerationBounds`'s doc comment claims it *\"proves\n127|the 1..256 bound in both directions against the number the specification\n128|declares: 256 accepted, 0 and 257 refused, on either side of the comparison.\"*\n129|It proves none of that. All four refusal cases pit a mutated generation against\n130|`base.Generation = atLimit`, so the values **differ** and\n131|`descriptor.Generation != binding.Generation` returns\n132|`terminal_backend_stale_generation` on its own — the bound never has to fire.\n133|\n134|- **M16** — widen `checkGeneration` upper bound 256 → 257: **survives.**\n135|- **M29** — widen it 256 → 100000: **survives.**\n136|- **M30** — delete the `len(generation) < 1` lower bound entirely: **survives.**\n137|\n138|`string[1..256]` therefore has zero test evidence, and the test's own comment is\n139|an unsupported claim about itself. The board's standing bar: *\"a pin over a\n140|documented claim must exercise the claim's SUBJECT, not only its parameter.\"*\n141|\n142|Fix: assert the bound where the two generations are **equal** (a 257-byte\n143|generation on both sides must still be refused; a 256-byte one on both sides\n144|must be admitted — the latter case already exists and is the only sound one).\n145|\n146|### F4 — four guards with no negative coverage; measured mutation ratio 20/32\n147|\n148|Reported as a ratio, not as prose. 32 mutants applied one at a time to\n149|`internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go` and `internal/config/validation.go`,\n150|each confirmed present (`count == 1`) before the run, each run against the owning\n151|package's full suite, sources restored from a byte-copy backup between mutants.\n152|**20 killed, 12 survived.** Full table in\n153|`.temp/TASK-260830-2xdt8t/mutation-results.json`.\n154|\n155|Surviving mutants beyond F2/F3:\n156|\n157|| Mutant | Guard | Why it survives |\n158|| --- | --- | --- |\n159|| M23 | `parseKind` closed `implementation_kind` vocabulary | admitting an arbitrary extra `Kind` breaks nothing — no test ever feeds an unknown kind. The `built-in kind for external path` case is caught earlier by the external-kind check in `RegisterExternal`, never by `parseKind`. |\n160|| M20 | `validatePlatforms` sorted-unique (`>=` → `>`) | no test feeds a duplicated platform |\n161|| M21 | `validatePlatforms` non-empty (`len == 0` removed) | no test feeds an empty platform set |\n162|| M22 | `validatePlatforms` upper bound (4 → 5) | no test feeds an over-long platform set |\n163|| M08 | `protocol_versions` upper bound (32 → 33) | `TestNewRefusesBadVersionTuples`'s `over 32 protocols` case builds 33 **identical** `\"1.0.0\"` entries, so it is killed by the sorted-unique rule, not by the count bound. The declared `semver[1..32]` maximum is unpinned. |\n164|| M24b | `DigestFile` regular-file refusal | `DigestFile(t.TempDir())` still errors with the guard neutered, because `os.ReadFile` fails EISDIR anyway. The class the guard exists for — FIFO, device, socket — is untested. This repository already documents that exact hazard at `README.md:1120`: *\"the only thing that can refuse; with it removed `os.Open` blocks indefinitely on a FIFO.\"* |\n165|| M28 | `terminalbackend.ParseID` on external-trust entries in `validateTerminal` | reverting that call to the old local pattern leaves the suite green, because the `strings.HasPrefix(entry.BackendID, \"ax.\")` line two statements later catches both tested inputs. The trust-entry half of the wiring is not load-bearing for any test. |\n166|\n167|`validatePlatforms` as a whole is deletable without reddening anything.\n168|\n169|For the record, the guards that **are** properly narrowed (killed): ID grammar\n170|widened to uppercase (M04) and to underscore separators (M05), ID byte bound\n171|128→129 (M03), reserved-namespace prefix widened (M01) and narrowed (M02),\n172|protocol major gate `!=1`→`<1` (M06), protocol sorted-unique `>=`→`>` (M07),\n173|`equalRecord` scalar members (M09–M11), digest-substitution check narrowed to\n174|7 bytes (M14), disabled-trust gate narrowed to one ID (M15), descriptor protocol\n175|comparison dropped (M17), descriptor generation comparison narrowed (M18),\n176|`needsDigest` narrowed to `local_program` (M19), `CheckVersionTuple` membership\n177|loop made always-true (M31), `RequireRestoreBinding` candidate gate narrowed\n178|(M32), config ax.-reservation removed (M25), config §6.2 Windows guard narrowed\n179|to WSL2 (M26), config `backend_id` `ParseID` wiring reverted (M27). Those are\n180|real, and M27 in particular proves the registry is a live production call site\n181|of configuration validation.\n182|\n183|### F5 — §6.2 was made real in a test and nowhere the gate can see it\n184|\n185|The task notes set this leaf a specific target: §6.2 is *\"the single `full`\n186|binding in the whole registry … discharged by a POSITIVE-path test only … the\n187|obvious thing to make real.\"*\n188|\n189|The producer wrote `TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows`, and the\n190|guard behind it is genuine — M26 (narrow the guard from `PlatformWindows` to\n191|`PlatformWSL2`) kills it. But the test is bound to nothing:\n192|\n193|- `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` is **not in the commit**\n194|  (`git show --stat b5cf5b5` lists 5 files; that file is not among them). The\n195|  §6.2 acceptance case still names only the positive test.\n196|- `README.md` is **not in the commit** either, and `README.md:1964-1966` still\n197|  reads: *\"One binding is `full` (Section 6.2, whose single clause is the\n198|  native-Windows `conpty` requirement, discharged by\n199|  `TestEveryPinnedReaderHasPositiveNativeWindowsAndWSL2Lanes`).\"*\n200|\n201|So the gate still reports the positive-path-only discharge the board asked this\n202|leaf to fix, and the repository's own coverage prose still makes that claim.\n203|The DoD line *\"README/doctor/capability evidence and specification traceability\n204|are updated without unsupported claims\"* is checked off against a commit that\n205|touches none of those files.\n206|\n207|Fix: add the negative case to the §6.2 acceptance case in\n208|`ownership.v0.5.0.json`, and update the README sentence to name both arms.\n209|\n210|## 3. The two producer decisions the board asked me to adjudicate — both UPHELD\n211|\n212|### D1 — `required_capabilities` keeps its empty default: the producer is right; record the audit finding as ANSWERED\n213|\n214|Judged on the pinned text, not on the audit's authority. Verified independently\n215|against the pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.md`:\n216|\n217|- `SPEC.md:2585` is the **sole** occurrence of \"platform lane minimum\" in the\n218|  12,665-line document: `| required_capabilities | Sorted unique closed\n219|  capability names; default platform lane minimum |`.\n220|- `grep -n \"minimum\"` returns 2467, **2585**, 2652, 6433, 6447, 6449, 6576,\n221|  11651, 11815, 12335, 12398, 12467, 12479 — none of the others enumerates a\n222|  per-platform capability set.\n223|- `grep -n \"lane\"` puts every other hit in §19.2 *Platform lanes* (11819–11848,\n224|  12017–12035, 12208, 12220), which is the **CI test-lane matrix** — a different\n225|  concept that enumerates no capability set either.\n226|\n227|No per-platform minimum is enumerated anywhere, so implementing one would invent\n228|a constraint — the failure this board has removed six times. Keeping the empty\n229|default is the only non-inventing choice. **The audit finding should be recorded\n230|as answered, not left open.**\n231|\n232|One correction the producer should carry into the rework. The LOGBOOK entry\n233|leans on `SPEC.md:2604-2605` (*\"Policy may further restrict\n234|capabilities/transports but cannot enable an unsupported claim\"*) to argue that\n235|\"empty enables nothing\". That sentence does not carry the argument:\n236|`required_capabilities` **restricts**, so an empty default is the *weaker* gate,\n237|not a neutral one — a non-empty minimum would refuse backends that empty admits.\n238|The honest framing is a **stated bound**: the clause is underspecified, the only\n239|implementable default is empty, and the reason is about the contract, not about\n240|effort. Restate the LOGBOOK decision that way and drop the 2604-2605\n241|justification.\n242|\n243|### D2 — §6.2 not extended to third-party IDs: correct reading, not a hole\n244|\n245|`SPEC.md:2415-2418` reads *\"terminal backend MUST be `conpty`\"* and sits inside\n246|§6.2, the Configuration 1.0.0/2.0.0 **example** section (the example document\n247|immediately above it at `SPEC.md:2400` is `backend = \"tmux\"`). It names the\n248|literal legacy token `conpty`, not the v3 canonical ID `ax.conpty`. The RFC 2119\n249|keyword therefore governs the v1/v2 `terminal.backend` field and its v3 image,\n250|both of which this repository implements and both of which are now enforced.\n251|\n252|§6.5 independently admits third-party IDs (`| backend_id | … valid registered\n253|ID |`) with manifest-bound platforms, and `validateTerminal` is consistent with\n254|that: `ax.tmux` is refused on Windows, `ax.conpty` off Windows, third-party IDs\n255|admitted on any platform when an enabled trust entry registers them. Declining\n256|to extend a legacy-vocabulary clause to third-party registered IDs is a correct\n257|reading of the pinned text, not a hole with a justification attached. The\n258|negative arm itself is real (M26 kills it).\n259|\n260|## 4. Board question 3 — is claiming no new ownership honest?\n261|\n262|Mostly yes, and for the right reason. `grep -rn terminalbackend --include='*.go'`\n263|returns exactly three files: the package, its test, and\n264|`internal/config/validation.go`. The config file calls **only** `ParseID`.\n265|`New`, `RegisterExternal`, `Resolve`, `IDs`, `DefaultForPlatform`,\n266|`RequireRestoreBinding`, `CheckVersionTuple`, `CheckProviderDescriptor` and\n267|`DigestFile` are called from nowhere in production. This repository builds no\n268|`ax` binary yet (`go list` shows two `main` packages, both internal codegen\n269|tools), so claiming §4.B/§7.A ownership for that surface would be an over-claim\n270|of the *\"a guard that no production path invokes has never run\"* kind. Declining\n271|to claim it is the honest call.\n272|\n273|The one arguable under-claim: the `ax.` namespace reservation on\n274|`terminal.backend_id` **is** enforced at a live production entry point\n275|(`EncodeCurrent`/`Decode` → `validateTerminal` → `ParseID`), M27 proves it is\n276|load-bearing, and it carries a real negative test. No binding was declared for\n277|it. That is secondary to F5 and can be handled with it — but if a binding is\n278|declared, note that the external-trust half of the same wiring is currently not\n279|load-bearing (M28) and must not be claimed alongside it.\n280|\n281|## 5. Also observed, not blocking\n282|\n283|- **Two independent implementations of the same declared scalar.** This leaf\n284|  correctly deleted `config.terminalBackendIDPattern` in favour of\n285|  `terminalbackend.ParseID`, but `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go:1230`\n286|  `requireTerminalBackendID` still carries its own copy of the identical grammar\n287|  and byte bound, and does **not** apply the `ax.` reservation — a\n288|  `terminal.created` payload may carry `ax.evil`. This is pre-existing and\n289|  already disclosed at `README.md:577-582` (*\"that is a registry and trust rule\n290|  rather than a payload constraint\"*), and a downstream consumer going through\n291|  `ParseID` fails closed on such an ID, so it is not a new regression. Worth a\n292|  deliberate decision now that a registry exists: either wire the second surface\n293|  or restate the disclosure as a bound naming the registry.\n294|- **`Registration.validate`'s `ExecutableDigest must be null` arm is\n295|  unreachable** from either production entry point: `New` only builds built-ins\n296|  with an empty digest, and `RegisterExternal` refuses any non-external kind\n297|  before `validate` runs. Dead, and consequently unmeasured.\n298|\n299|## 6. Evidence\n300|\n301|All under `.temp/TASK-260830-2xdt8t/` in the story worktree, attached to the board:\n302|\n303|| Artifact | Contents |\n304|| --- | --- |\n305|| `review-aliasing-probe.log` | the three F1 aliasing probes, run against the real exported API |\n306|| `zz_review_probe_test.go` | probes A–C (Resolve aliasing, RegisterExternal retention, shared built-in slice) |\n307|| `zz_review_probe2_test.go` | probe D (drift gate reports duplicate instead of drift) |\n308|| `mutation-results.json` | all 28 round-1 mutants with status |\n309|| `mutate.py` | the harness: byte-copy backup, `count == 1` presence check, restore between mutants |\n310|\n311|Probe files were run inside `internal/terminalbackend/` and moved back out;\n312|`git status --short` is clean and `HEAD` is unchanged at `b5cf5b5`. No\n313|production code was modified by this review.\n314|\n315|## 7. Routing\n316|\n317|`to-dev`. F1 and F2 are one fix (defensive copies plus same-length drift cases).\n318|F3, F4 and F5 are independent and each small. D1 and D2 need no code change —\n319|D1 needs the LOGBOOK justification restated as a stated bound and the audit\n320|finding recorded as answered."}}}
