{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c70b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e75-bbc6-7500-a9cb-f70149b63e89"},"sequence":480,"recorded_at":1780531400000955,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c"},"call_id":"call_01a06e76d67e78f281ae9dea88ed551a","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-18-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '2490,2560p' internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\",\n  \"description\": \"View 6.2 ownership binding entry\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"        \\\"declaration\\\": \\\"Load\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"acceptance_cases\\\": [\\n        \\\"config-versioned-readers\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"unevidenced\\\",\\n      \\\"gap\\\": \\\"Load resolves and reads the Section 6.1 configuration roots, but the clause requiring those resolved roots across the CLI, service, RPC server and provider host has no implementation, and no clause of Section 6.1 is enumerated.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"section_binding\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\n        \\\"section:6.2\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"production\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/config/schema.go\\\",\\n        \\\"declaration\\\": \\\"Decode\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"acceptance_cases\\\": [\\n        \\\"config-versioned-readers\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"full\\\",\\n      \\\"clauses\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"6.2#1\\\",\\n          \\\"line\\\": 2417,\\n          \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"MUST be <code>conpty</code>. WSL2 configuration uses Linux paths and <code>platform = \\\\\\\"wsl2\\\\\\\"</code>.\\\",\\n          \\\"acceptance_cases\\\": [\\n            \\\"config-versioned-readers\\\"\\n          ]\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"section_binding\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\n        \\\"section:6.3\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"production\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/config/validation.go\\\",\\n        \\\"declaration\\\": \\\"validateConfiguration\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"acceptance_cases\\\": [\\n        \\\"config-versioned-readers\\\",\\n        \\\"config-current-writer\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"unevidenced\\\",\\n      \\\"gap\\\": \\\"validateConfiguration enforces many Section 6.3 field constraints, but no clause of Section 6.3 is enumerated against an acceptance case, so the registry makes no clause-level claim about its obligations.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"section_binding\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\n        \\\"section:6.4\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"production\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/config/migration.go\\\",\\n        \\\"declaration\\\": \\\"Migrate\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"acceptance_cases\\\": [\\n        \\\"config-versioned-readers\\\",\\n        \\\"config-durable-migration\\\",\\n        \\\"config-durable-migration-os-entry\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"unevidenced\\\",\\n      \\\"gap\\\": \\\"Migrate writes the Section 6.4 durable migration with an owner-only backup and atomic replacement, but no clause of Section 6.4 is enumerated against an acceptance case.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"section_binding\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\n        \\\"section:6.5\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"production\\\": {\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 2261,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 566,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c70d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e75-bbc6-7500-a9cb-f70149b63e89"},"sequence":481,"recorded_at":1780531400000957,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4e2efeb9-af88-4b92-82e3-c4ef7a43be2c"},"call_id":"call_01a06e76d8357ff1a9195c587ddc204d","text":"Read text file `LOGBOOK.md`.\n1|# Flight Logbook\n2|\n3|> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\n4|> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\n5|\n6|## 2026-09-04\n7|\n8|### 2147 — Terminal backend registry: the two board-disclosed findings decided without inventing constraints\n9|\n10|- SCOPE: new `internal/terminalbackend/` (registry, canonical IDs, version tuples, trust, duplicate/drift refusal, restore binding, §7.A descriptor check), `internal/config/validation.go` wiring, new `internal/config/terminal_registry_wiring_test.go`. TASK-260830-2xdt8t, spec v0.5.0 §4.B, §4.1, §6.5, §7.A. Signed commit on story branch, worktree clean, not pushed.\n11|- DECISION (finding 1): `required_capabilities` keeps its empty default. SPEC.md:2585 (\"default platform lane minimum\") is the sole occurrence of the phrase in the 12,665-line SPEC; no per-platform minimum set is enumerated anywhere, so implementing one would invent a constraint. Empty enables nothing per SPEC.md:2604-2605 (\"Policy may further restrict capabilities/transports but cannot enable an unsupported claim\"); per-operation capability dependencies (§4.C/§4.D) still gate activation. No code change.\n12|- DECISION (finding 2): §6.2 \"terminal backend MUST be conpty\" on native Windows (SPEC.md:2415-2418) governs the v1/v2 `terminal.backend` field and is enforced across versions; added the missing legacy-arm pin `TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows` (v1 tmux-on-Windows refuses, conpty loads to `ax.conpty`). Deliberately NOT extended to third-party IDs: §6.5 admits them as \"valid registered ID\" with manifest-bound platforms.\n13|- EVIDENCE: `go test ./...` exit 0 (14 packages), `go vet ./...` exit 0, gofmt clean; terminalbackend 89.3%, config 94.7% statement coverage. Every refusal gate narrowed (grammar widened one rule at a time; drift changes one record member at a time); `TestErrorPredicatesAreExclusive` proves one wire code per refusal; duplicate/drift refusals assert the admitted record is unchanged. Wire codes verified against the pinned catalog vocabulary in-test.\n14|- BOUND: registry is process-local memory — no durable-state crash-recovery evidence applies; duplicate/drift non-clobber assertions are the idempotency evidence. Manifest/Probe schemas, capability evidence, lifecycle ops, legacy v0.4.3 translation are sibling tasks, untouched.\n15|\n16|## 2026-09-03\n17|\n18|### 1632 — The generalisation was written down one round earlier and still not executed: a list is a forced traversal, prose is a memory\n19|\n20|- SCOPE: new `internal/cliresult/guards_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/guard_inventory_test.go`, one production fix in `internal/cliresult/client.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-7 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §1.6, §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §17.2.\n21|- ROOT CAUSE: the `ErrUnregisteredExitStatus` gate in `cliresult.Read` was driven at four sampled values — `{1, 42, -1, 255}` — while its twin `axerror.decodeExitStatus`, which has the same shape and the same domain, was swept over 0..255 in the round-6 rework one file away. Restricting the reader gate to exactly its four covered values left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages, as did sparing 127 and 137 — the two statuses a wrapper most plausibly returns when `ax` never ran. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head with the delete-only control KILLED in the same run.\n22|- FINDING: entry 1447 below states the exact generalisation this round violated — \"when a class of 'proven at one sample' is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named\". It was written and then not executed. The orchestrator asked for the enumerated list, accepted a sweep without it on the reasoning that the table was a means rather than an end, and the omission it would have made visible cost a whole review round. A generalisation recorded as prose is a memory; a list is a traversal. Working from memory closes the guard you were told about.\n23|- FIX: `TestTheRefusalGuardInventoryIsDerivedFromTheReaderSource` parses every refusal site out of `internal/cliresult/client.go` with `go/ast` and requires a bijection with a published README table — twelve rows, each naming its function, sentinel, refusal marker, domain-evidence class from a closed vocabulary, and the test declarations that measure it. A guard added to the reader with no row is a red; a row whose marker no longer resolves is a red. A second table enumerates the guards this leaf added or reordered outside that file, `decodeExitStatus` required in it by name, because the twin one file away is exactly what a single-file table would have missed again.\n24|- FINDING: the traversal immediately produced three more instances of the class, none of which any reviewer had named. The `ErrForeignDocument` branch was covered by one schema identifier, so restricting it to that identifier and routing every other foreign schema into `readSuccess` survived the suite. The \"schema member is not a string\" guard was covered by the number `5`, so restricting it to JSON numbers survived. Both are the round-6 shape: not an admission bypass, a change in WHICH FACT the machine client is told. The presence guard for a missing `schema` member survives an arbitrary predicate keyed on the exit status, which is the class round 4 declined to block on and is recorded as a stated bound rather than fixed.\n25|- FIX (production, the only behaviour change this round): `encoding/json` admits JSON `null` into a `string` and yields `\"\"`, so a document whose `schema` member was `null` had been answered as a FOREIGN CONTRACT carrying the schema `\"\"` — a claim that some other contract owns the document, when what is true is that this one is not readable. Found only because the type guard's domain was swept rather than sampled. The guard now checks the raw token alongside the unmarshal.\n26|- DECISION: the round-6 reviewer asked whether `exitStatusIsNotEnough` should distinguish \"this status carries no registered code\" from \"this status is not in the table\", since it computes `len(fanIn[status])` with no membership check and would report a map miss as a measured 0. The answer is not a defensive branch: a branch no production path can take promises nothing and is never measured. `TestTheFanInSentenceCannotReportAMapMissAsAMeasurement` proves the fabrication unreachable instead — `documentSchema` has exactly one caller and it is `Read`, the gate precedes that call in `Read`'s body by AST position, and every (registered version, registered failure status) pair measures at least one code, so a 0 there could only ever have been a miss. Moving the gate behind the discriminator reddens it.\n27|- FINDING (reviewer observation, disclosed rather than fixed): the §1.6 common-data-model gate does NOT enforce the number half of that section. `Read` admits a Structured Error whose `details` carry `1.5`, which SPEC.md forbids as integers inside the IEEE 754 safe-integer range. The gate's doc comment enumerates what it owns honestly; its opening sentence and README's matching phrasing overstated it. §1.6's number rule is bound to `internal/scalar` and left unevidenced there, so nothing false is claimed in the traceability projection. The admission is now ASSERTED by a test, so closing the gap reddens the pin and forces the disclosure to be updated in the same change — a disclosure with no pin decays into a paragraph nobody re-reads.\n28|- FINDING (reviewer observation, correction to the record): three mutants were dismissed in round 5 on the grounds that \"none is a registered code\". `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` IS registered at Structured Error 1.3.0 exit 6 (`catalog_gen.go:541`), and sparing it in `ExitCodeFor`'s version scoping survives on a clean tree. The conclusion holds for a different reason — the same narrowing on `observation_gap` is killed by `TestExitCodeForRefusesVersionAndCodeDrift`, so the class has a covered representative. The dismissal stands; the sentence behind it did not. A survivor dismissed with a wrong reason is worse than one dismissed with none, because the reason is what the next round trusts.\n29|- DECISION: reviewer observation O3 was closed by measurement rather than by the stated bound the reviewer offered. `verifyEnvelopeIdentity`'s major comparison was proven at major 2 alone; it is now swept over majors 0..64 through `Read` with the bound stated for the tail. Where a domain can be swept cheaply, a stated bound is a bound about effort, and this leaf has published the rule that a bound must be about the contract.\n30|- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All eight new tests register against the existing `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the registration was probed by renaming a declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\n31|- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 63 to 86 mutants — 85 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 64 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green after every batch. Supersedes the \"63 mutants at 62 killed\" figure in the 1447 entry below. One mutant on the §1.6 disclosure was DISCARDED as equivalent and replaced: disabling the assertion in the test changes no outcome while the gap is open, so the replacement closes the gap in production and requires the pin to redden. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\n32|\n33|### 1447 — The exit status a document declares is a member, not a fact: a guard proven on one code let a relabelled envelope forge a retry claim\n34|\n35|- SCOPE: new `internal/axerror/agreement_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/relabel_test.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-6 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — every round-6 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\n36|- ROOT CAUSE: the code-to-exit-status agreement in `axerror.decodeBody`, `if exitCode != expectedExit`, was covered by exactly one row of `TestDecodeRefusesClosedShapeViolations`: `observation_gap` driven at exit 5 instead of its own exit 9. One code, one wrong status, out of 752 ordered (code, wrong-status) pairs at 1.0.0 and 1,744 at 1.3.0. Narrowing the guard to `code == \"observation_gap\"` — restricting it to exactly the pair that row drives — left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages; so did sparing `policy_refused` or `authentication_failed` individually. Only deleting the guard reddened anything. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written.\n37|- FINDING: this was a reachable bypass and not only an unmeasured ratio. `code` and `exit_code` are two independent members of one document, and this guard is the sole thing binding them. The retryability refusal this package publishes keys on the EXIT STATUS for three whole classes — 7 authorization, 16 policy refusal, 130 interrupt — so relabelling a code out of its own class asks that refusal about the wrong class and it stays silent. Under the narrowing, `{\"code\":\"authentication_failed\",\"exit_code\":9,\"retryable\":true}` was ADMITTED by `cliresult.Read` at exit status 9, and the caller received a `Reading` naming an authorization failure with `Retryable() == true`. The §14.2 exit-status corroboration cannot see it, because the process really did exit with the relabelled status.\n38|- FINDING: this is the FOURTH guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231 and the command-tag agreement in entry 1421. Each round closed the guards it was shown and did not sweep the sibling on the same reading path. The generalisation, stated once: when a class of \"proven at one sample\" is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named.\n39|- FIX: `TestTheCodeToExitStatusAgreementIsMeasuredOverTheWholeCodeRegistry` drives all 316 registered codes across the 4 registered versions at all 17 registered failure statuses — 5,372 rows. The agreeing status must be ADMITTED and each of the other sixteen refused with this guard's own sentence naming both numbers, so a pair settled by `decodeExitStatus`, by the closed member set, or by the retryability gate does not count as coverage of it. Per-version totals are pinned at 752, 1,056, 1,504 and 1,744 and re-derived from `CodesFor` and `CodesByExitStatus`.\n40|- DECISION: close the bypass at the production entry point as well as at the decoder, because they are different claims. `TestRelabellingACodeOutOfItsExitClassCannotForgeARetryClaimThroughRead` takes the 7 codes of Structured Error 1.0.0 whose own class carries an exit-keyed retryability refusal, relabels each into all 14 statuses that carry none, and requires `Read` to refuse all 98. The control in the other direction is required in the same loop — the same code at its own status with `retryable: true` must be refused by the retryability gate — which is what makes the relabelling a bypass of something rather than a rewrite of nothing.\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
